2. Improved Brute Force

Instead of recalculating minimum each time, update it while expanding the subarray.

Idea
start = i
expand j
update min = min(min, arr[j])
Time Complexity

O(n²)

Code
class Solution {
    public int sumSubarrayMins(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        int sum = 0;

        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++){
            int min = arr[i];

            for(int j = i; j < n; j++){
                min = Math.min(min, arr[j]);
                sum += min;
            }
        }

        return sum;
    }
}
3. Monotonic Stack (Optimal)

Instead of checking subarrays, calculate how many subarrays an element is the minimum for.

Formula

contribution = arr[i] × left × right

Where

left = distance to previous smaller element

right = distance to next smaller element

Time Complexity

O(n)

This is the best possible.

You cannot do better than O(n) because you must read every element at least once.

Final Comparison
Approach	Time	Space
Brute Force	O(n³)	O(1)
Improved Brute	O(n²)	O(1)
Monotonic Stack	O(n)	O(n)

✅ Conclusion

The stack solution is already optimal.

No algorithm can solve it faster than O(n).

